Reading overlaid on the real NOAA frame; packet anchored to dot centers

TWO DEFECTS REPORTED FROM THE LIVE PAGE, both real:

1. The travelling packet floated below every node dot. Cause: it targeted
   offsetTop + offsetHeight/2, the middle of the whole ROW, while the dot
   sits near the row's top. Both the packet and the spine fill now measure
   the .tl-dot rect itself, so the anchor is the dot center by construction.
   Verified to the decimal on the served page: packet center 7.5, 266.3
   against a dot center of 7.5, 266.3.

   Debugging note worth keeping: the first probe showed the packet 139px off
   AFTER the fix, which looked like a worse bug. It was the probe: CSS
   transitions do not advance in a tab that never paints, so the frozen
   mid-transition value read as a misposition. Bypassing the transition for
   one frame showed the true landing spot. Same non-painting-tab family as
   the rAF sweep and the screenshot stalls.

2. The dual-pane evidence images rendered small, and two small frames side
   by side said less than one big one. MERGED: the actual NOAA frame now
   renders at full column width with the imagery agent's reading overlaid
   as markers ON it. Pulsing amber rings on the two [low] sites, gray dots
   on [not present], labeled chips that flip near the right edge, appearing
   when the imagery stage completes during the replay.

   Positions live in core/sites.py, measured by eye off the visible sample
   clusters of the committed frame, and BOTH the payload and the on-page
   caption say "approximate positions" in as many words. An eyeballed
   overlay that says it is eyeballed beats a precise-looking schematic that
   corresponds to nothing. A test pins core/sites.py and the schematic
   renderer's table together so the two readings can never drift, and the
   tool artifact stays on the page below the advisory, smaller.

111 tests pass.
